WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.820
Beth: A constructive environment is
an environment where there is not an

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:10.380
overload of sensory stimuli be it visual,
auditory, olfactory or related to space

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:14.819
and an individual with autism has to
feel safe and comfortable to be able

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.149
to communicate or interact socially.

00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:20.570
Let’s look at some of the issues,
starting with the lighting

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:22.740
is it natural or artificial?

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.520
Joe: Lighting plays a large
part in the sensory experiences

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:27.990
of many autistic people.

00:00:28.429 --> 00:00:32.850
We now know that fluorescent lights can
be very distracting even to the point

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:36.700
of debilitating for some people because
they can see the lights flickering.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:39.760
Beth: Is the environment
cluttered or minimalist.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.250
Many people with autism need
space around them and feel

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.319
overwhelmed by crowds or clutter.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:49.499
They can feel hemmed in by corridors
and need to know there is a quick

00:00:49.519 --> 00:00:53.149
escape route or a place they can
remove themselves to which is calmer.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:57.500
Joe: Individuals with Autism can
become overwhelmed by too much

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.040
visual information to process.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.360
Patterns can be distracting
and overwhelming and can

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.019
even cause visual distortion.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.229
Beth: What about sounds
internal or external?

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:11.710
Are there any regular ‘external’
sounds, such as traffic, children

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:13.260
playing or construction noise?

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:18.020
Are there regular ‘internal' sounds,
such as ticking clocks, machines

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.130
humming, music or radio noise?

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.670
Joe: Is it possible to
reduce these sounds?

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.810
Many individuals with autism
can hear sounds at many decibels

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.330
above those others can hear.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:33.190
They hear sounds that are further away
and the intensity can often be deafening.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.600
Beth: Are there any strange smells?

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.350
There may be smells from outside
permeating through the walls

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:42.890
or from within such as cleaning
products, perfumes or foods.

00:01:43.929 --> 00:01:48.060
Some people with autism find smells
so overwhelming that they cause

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:52.560
extreme nausea and some can even
smell something for a long time after

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.190
it has been removed from the room.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:59.480
Joe: To focus on social interaction and
communication many individuals with autism

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:03.970
need to have reduced sensory distractions
to be able to listen to conversations,

00:02:04.309 --> 00:02:08.533
to respond appropriately, keep calm and
relate to what is being said, rather

00:02:08.533 --> 00:02:21.814
than be distracted to some extent or in
extremes suffering from sensory overload.

